          "type": "concept",
          "title": "The City Dionysia Festival",
          "content": "The **City Dionysia** (also called the **Great Dionysia**) was held annually in **late March or early April**, the month of **Elaphebolion**, when sea travel resumed and allied tributes could arrive.\n\nThe festival lasted approximately **five days**:\n- **Day 1:** *Proagon* (preview) and the *pompe* (procession carrying a statue of Dionysus Eleuthereus)\n- **Days 2–4:** Three full days of tragedy, each featuring one tragedian's **tetralogy** (3 tragedies + 1 satyr play)\n- **Day 5:** Five comedies\n\nA wealthy citizen called a **choregos** financed each playwright's chorus as a public service (*leitourgia*). This system bound art directly to the city's elite financial class.",
          "visual": {
            "type": "diagram",
            "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"70\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"36\">The City Dionysia</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"115\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"24\">Five days in Elaphebolion (late March)</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"180\" width=\"940\" height=\"140\" fill=\"rgba(236,72,153,0.12)\" stroke=\"#ec4899\" stroke-width=\"2\" rx=\"12\"/><text x=\"120\" y=\"230\" fill=\"#ec4899\" font-size=\"26\" font-weight=\"bold\">Day 1: Proagon and Pompe</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"270\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Playwrights introduce their works without costume.</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"300\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Procession carries Dionysus's statue to the theatre.</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"350\" width=\"940\" height=\"380\" fill=\"rgba(99,102,241,0.12)\" stroke=\"#6366f1\" stroke-width=\"2\" rx=\"12\"/><text x=\"120\" y=\"400\" fill=\"#6366f1\" font-size=\"26\" font-weight=\"bold\">Days 2-4: Tragedy Competition</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"445\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Each day: one tragedian's tetralogy</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"490\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-size=\"22\">Morning: Tragedy 1</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"525\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-size=\"22\">Midday: Tragedy 2</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"560\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-size=\"22\">Afternoon: Tragedy 3</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"600\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" font-size=\"22\">Late afternoon: Satyr play (comic relief)</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"650\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"18\">Three tragedians competed across the three days.</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"680\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"18\">Total: 9 tragedies + 3 satyr plays viewed.</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"710\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"18\">Spectators sat dawn to dusk on stone benches.</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"760\" width=\"940\" height=\"140\" fill=\"rgba(20,184,166,0.12)\" stroke=\"#14b8a6\" stroke-width=\"2\" rx=\"12\"/><text x=\"120\" y=\"810\" fill=\"#14b8a6\" font-size=\"26\" font-weight=\"bold\">Day 5: Comedy</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"850\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Five comic poets compete with one play each.</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"880\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Aristophanes won here repeatedly later in the century.</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"930\" width=\"940\" height=\"120\" fill=\"rgba(255,215,0,0.10)\" stroke=\"#ffd700\" stroke-width=\"2\" rx=\"12\"/><text x=\"120\" y=\"980\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-size=\"26\" font-weight=\"bold\">Closing Ceremony</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"1020\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Winners announced; ivy crowns awarded by judges.</text></svg>",
            "caption": "Five days of ritual, tragedy, satyr play, and comedy — a structured rhythm of civic emotion."
          }
