          "type": "concept",
          "title": "Sophocles: A Life in Outline",
          "content": "**Sophocles (c. 497 – 406 BCE)** lived nearly the entire 5th century BCE — the so-called **Golden Age** of Athens. Born in the deme of **Colonus** (the setting of his final play), he died at roughly **90 years old**, two years before Athens lost the Peloponnesian War.\n\nBiographical anchors:\n- **480 BCE:** as a teenager, led the chorus celebrating the naval victory at **Salamis**\n- **468 BCE:** at age ~29, **defeated Aeschylus** for first prize at the Dionysia (his debut victory)\n- Served as **strategos** (general) alongside Pericles in 441 BCE during the Samian War\n- Served as a **Hellenotamias** (imperial treasurer) in 443 BCE\n- Was a priest of the healing hero **Halon**\n\nHe wrote roughly **120 plays**; only **seven** survive complete: *Ajax*, *Antigone*, *Trachiniae*, *Oedipus Tyrannos*, *Electra*, *Philoctetes*, *Oedipus at Colonus*.",
